---
import UseCaseLayout from "../../layouts/UseCaseLayout.astro";
import { whoCanEdit } from "../../data/demo-answers.ts";
import { whyCantSee } from "../../data/page-answers.ts";
---

<UseCaseLayout
  title="Why Can't This User See the Record? Salesforce Access"
  description="Find out why a Salesforce user can't see or edit a record, and who can. Profiles, permission sets, org-wide defaults and sharing rules, checked in order."
  path="/use-cases/sharing-troubleshooting"
  heading="Why can’t this user see it?"
  lede="Access tickets arrive as one line and turn into an afternoon in Setup. Ask the question in plain words and get the setting that decides it, plus everything else that was checked."
  crumb="Sharing and access"
  audience="admins, support teams and architects"
  short="Ask your AI assistant “why can’t Installers see Invoices?”. sf-intelligence checks access the way Salesforce applies it: object permissions, system permissions, org-wide defaults, sharing rules, then restriction rules. It names the setting that decides the answer, and marks anything that depends on record data as “unknown” instead of guessing."
  answers={[whyCantSee, whoCanEdit]}
  answerIntro="Two access questions asked of Verdant, the demo org: one from a support ticket, one from an access review."
  asks={[
    "Why can’t Installers see Invoices?",
    "Who can edit Invoices?",
    "Who can edit the Salary field on Employee?",
    "What can the Read Only profile actually do?",
    "Which permission sets give delete on Opportunity?",
  ]}
  checks={[
    "<strong>Object and field permissions</strong> from profiles, permission sets and permission set groups, including muting",
    "<strong>System permissions</strong> such as View All Data and Modify All Data",
    "<strong>Org-wide defaults</strong> and the role hierarchy",
    "<strong>Owner and criteria sharing rules</strong>, and restriction and scoping rules",
    "<strong>Who it leaves out</strong>, and why, when you filter by access level",
  ]}
  limits={[
    "Who is assigned which permission set (that’s record data). The optional live mode can read it",
    "Manual shares, account teams and sharing sets for a specific record",
    "Whether a criteria sharing rule matches one particular record",
    "Access changes made since your last refresh",
  ]}
  tools={["why_cant_user_see_record", "who_can_access_object", "field_access_audit", "effective_permissions", "user_ability", "layout_for_user"]}
  related={[
    { href: "/use-cases/impact-analysis", label: "Impact analysis", note: "before you change a permission set" },
    { href: "/use-cases/agent-readiness-audit", label: "Agent-readiness audit", note: "what an AI agent could see" },
    { href: "/trust", label: "Trust and security", note: "how answers are checked" },
    { href: "/ai-safety", label: "Is AI safe on my org?" },
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: "Can it tell me whether one specific user can see one specific record?",
      a: "Not from the snapshot alone. Record ownership, manual shares and team membership are record data, not metadata. The answer marks those steps as unknown. If you turn on the optional read-only live mode, it can ask Salesforce directly for one record.",
    },
    {
      q: "Does it change permissions?",
      a: "No. It tells you which profile, permission set or rule to change. Making the change stays a deliberate step in Setup or a deployment.",
    },
    {
      q: "Why is a general AI assistant bad at this?",
      a: "It knows how Salesforce sharing works in general and will describe a plausible cascade with made-up profile names. sf-intelligence knows which profiles, permission sets and rules exist in your org, and says which ones it couldn’t check.",
    },
  ]}
  cta={{ question: whyCantSee.question, related: { href: "/demo#demo-who-can-edit", label: "See who can edit Invoices" } }}
  dateModified="2026-10-10"
>
  <h2>The setting that decides it</h2>
  <p>Salesforce access isn’t one setting. It’s a sequence, and a ticket stays open because the answer can sit at any step. The answer walks the steps in the order Salesforce applies them and names the one that decided the outcome. In the demo org, the Installer profile has no Read permission on Invoice, so nothing further down, not even a sharing rule, can show the record. That’s the one thing to fix.</p>
  <h2>“Unknown” is an answer</h2>
  <p>Some steps depend on the record itself: who owns it, whether someone shared it by hand, whether a criteria rule matches its values. The snapshot holds your org’s setup, not its records, so those steps come back as “unknown” for you to check. They are never quietly resolved in whichever direction makes the answer look complete.</p>
</UseCaseLayout>
